// A gate on an output field whose value is a STRING, not a boolean.
//
// This exists because the checker got it wrong. Every output field used to be modelled as a
// boolean, so `output.result: "ok"` could never match anything the model could produce, and the
// permit below was reported **VACUOUS** — a permit that works perfectly, declared inert.
//
//     python tests/strands/vacuity.py tests/policies/string_output.dw
//     ==> permit #2  action == Read   live   witness: Login -> Read
//
// A false VACUOUS is the one wrong answer this tool must not give: it tells someone a working
// control is dead, and the obvious response is to delete it. The fix was to derive each field's
// domain from the literals the policy actually names rather than assuming a type — mutation-checked
// by ignoring those literals, which brings the false VACUOUS straight back.
//
// Eight output binds in Dogwood's own corpus compare against a string, so this was not a corner
// nobody reaches.

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Login",
    resource
);

permit (
    principal,
    action == Anchor::Action::"Read",
    resource
)
when temporal {
    formerly within 1h Anchor::Action::"Login"::response{ output.result: "ok" }
};
